.Dd August 14, 2026
.Dt PAM_STNS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pam_stns
.Nd authenticate a password against an STNS directory
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm /usr/local/lib/pam/pam_stns.so
.Op Cm debug
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a PAM module that checks a password against the hash an
.Lk https://stns.jp/ STNS
API server holds for the user.
.Pp
.Xr ldapstns 8
answers who somebody is.
This answers whether a password is theirs, which on macOS is asked through a
different system entirely - and, unlike on the BSDs, through two of them:
.Bl -column "Open Directory authentication" "the login window" -offset indent
.It Sy PAM Ta Xr sshd 8 , Xr su 1 , Xr sudo 8
.It Sy Open Directory Ta the login window
.El
.Pp
This module covers the first and not the second.
.Sh THE LOGIN WINDOW
Worth stating plainly, because it is the sort of thing that is otherwise found
out at a locked screen:
.Em a directory user set up this way can log in over ssh and not at the
.Em keyboard .
.Pp
.Xr dsconfigldap 8
configures a node with the Open Directory Server template, whose authentication
modules are
.Sy AppleODClientLDAP
and
.Sy AppleODClientPWS
- Password Server and Kerberos.
.Xr ldapstns 8
speaks neither, and the alternative it could speak, a simple LDAP bind, is not
what that template asks for.
So the login window authenticates against something that is not there.
.Pp
Where the screen matters, the account wants to be local, with STNS supplying
only the SSH keys; see
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8 .
.Sh SETTING IT UP
The module is named by absolute path.
.Pa /usr/lib/pam
is inside the signed system volume and nothing can be added to it, so there is
nowhere else for a third-party module to live:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# /etc/pam.d/sshd
auth       optional       pam_krb5.so use_kcminit
auth       sufficient     /usr/local/lib/pam/pam_stns.so try_first_pass
auth       required       pam_opendirectory.so try_first_pass
.Ed
.Pp
.Cm sufficient
before
.Xr pam_opendirectory 8
rather than after it: a local account is answered by Open Directory without the
API being asked at all when the module is listed after, but a directory user
would then have already been refused.
Listed before, a local account falls through - the module returns
.Sy PAM_USER_UNKNOWN
for a name the directory does not hold, which continues the stack - and a
directory user is answered here.
.Pp
.Cm try_first_pass
is handled by
.Xr pam_get_authtok 3
and means what it always means: use the password an earlier module already
collected rather than prompting again.
.Pp
The same three lines work in
.Pa /etc/pam.d/su
and
.Pa /etc/pam.d/sudo .
.Pp
The only option this module reads itself is
.Cm debug ,
which logs a line on success as well as on failure.
.Sh HASHES
The comparison is done by the same library
.Xr ldapstns 8
links, which carries its own SHA-256 crypt and SHA-512 crypt.
It has to:
.Xr crypt 3
on macOS understands traditional DES and nothing else, and - the more
dangerous half - does not say so.
Handed
.Ql $6$salt$... ,
it takes
.Ql $6
as a two character salt and returns a perfectly ordinary looking DES hash.
A module that compared that would refuse every password with nothing anywhere
to say why.
.Pp
So
.Ql $6$
and
.Ql $5$
work here.
.Ql $2b$
does not, on this system, and is refused with
.Dq no usable password hash
in the log rather than as a wrong password.
.Sh SECURITY
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Failure is always a refusal
Every outcome that is not a positive match refuses.
The reason goes to
.Xr syslogd 8 ,
and the caller is told only which of the four kinds it was.
.It The password goes to the machine, not to the API
Only the hash is fetched; the comparison happens here.
The API is never sent a password and never asked whether one is correct.
.It The on-disk cache and the circuit breaker are turned off
Whatever
.Xr stns.conf 5
says.
The cache would be written as whoever called PAM and read as somebody else, and
the breaker would suppress the next minute of logins after one failed request -
the right trade when every process on the machine is asking, and the wrong one
when the question is whether to let a person in.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /usr/local/etc/stns/client/stns.conf -compact
.It Pa /usr/local/lib/pam/pam_stns.so
The module.
On Apple Silicon, where Homebrew installs into
.Pa /opt/homebrew ,
it is
.Pa /opt/homebrew/lib/pam/pam_stns.so .
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/stns/client/stns.conf
The STNS API client configuration, shared with
.Xr ldapstns 8
and
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8 .
.It Pa /etc/pam.d
Where a policy naming this module goes.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL -compact
.It Dv PAM_SUCCESS
The password matched the hash the directory holds.
.It Dv PAM_AUTH_ERR
It did not, or the account is locked, or its hash is of a kind this system
cannot read.
.It Dv PAM_USER_UNKNOWN
The directory does not hold that name, or the name is outside the character set
STNS permits.
The stack carries on, which is what lets a local account through.
.It Dv PAM_AUTHINFO_UNAVAIL
The API server could not be asked.
Deliberately not
.Dv PAM_AUTH_ERR :
a module that reported an outage as a wrong password would turn one into an
account lockout wherever a policy counts failures.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr su 1 ,
.Xr stns.conf 5 ,
.Xr ldapstns 8 ,
.Xr pam 8 ,
.Xr sshd 8 ,
.Xr stns-key-wrapper 8
.Sh AUTHORS
.An zakinko Aq Mt zakinko@snowrabbit.org
.Sh CAVEATS
Authentication needs the API server, every time.
There is no cached credential and deliberately no fallback to one: a password
checked against something this machine remembered is a password checked against
something that may since have been revoked.
An account that has to work while the directory is unreachable wants to be
local.
